QB.add(22,[{"id":22001,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which definition of a social role is correct?","o":["The expectations of how someone is supposed to behave","The legal duties attached to someone’s job title","A person’s fixed personality type","A status assigned once at birth and never changed","A diagnosis given by a doctor"],"e":"A social role <b>defines the expectations of how someone is supposed to behave</b>. Features: <b>dynamic</b>; <b>temporary or fixed</b>; happens when <b>interaction occurs</b> and we enter relationships; <b>playing</b> roles; <b>multiple</b> roles; importance of <b>context/culture</b>.","w":["Not legal.","Not personality.","Roles are dynamic.","Not."],"k":"Social role = behavioural expectations.","rn":[["","What is a social role?","What is a social role?","Expected way someone should behave"]]},{"id":22002,"t":"Social Roles","s":"A first-year medical student notices they behave differently on placement than with friends. Which feature of social roles explains this?","o":["Playing roles (“masking”)","Ascribed roles that cannot be changed by the student","Role loss on starting medical school","Adopting the sick role on placement","Secondary gains from being a student"],"e":"<b>Playing roles = “masking”</b>: we are all masking all the time, behaving as the social norm for a role requires. Medical students are now bound by <b>professional standards</b>; patients will see them as health professionals.","w":["Medical student is acquired.","Not loss.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Playing roles = masking.","rn":[["","What does \"playing roles\" mean?","What does \"playing roles\" mean?","Behaving differently in different situations; Putting on a front to match the social norm"],["","Social role features: how are they played?","Social role features: how are they played?","Playing roles; Multiple roles; Importance of context/culture"]]},{"id":22003,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which example best illustrates that social roles depend on context/culture?","o":["A UK wife’s role now vs 50 years ago or in the Philippines","A daughter’s role, which stays the same in every culture and every era","A medical student’s role lasts about five years","Retiring from work removes a role","Becoming a carer adds a role"],"e":"<b>Context/culture:</b> the role of a <b>wife</b> in the UK now is very different from 50 years ago, and very different from the Philippines.","w":["Context changes roles.","Temporary.","Loss.","Not context."],"k":"Roles vary by culture and era.","rn":[["","Why does context/culture matter for a social role?","Why does context/culture matter for a social role?","Role of a wife differs: UK now vs 50 years ago; Also differs between countries, e.g. the Philippines"]]},{"id":22004,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Being a medical student is described as “temporary” and “dynamic”. What do these mean here?","o":["It lasts about five years and keeps changing","It lasts a lifetime and keeps changing","It lasts about five years but never changes","It is assigned at birth and fixed","It exists only in contact with patients"],"e":"<b>Dynamic</b>: not fixed, always changing. <b>Temporary</b>: you are only a medical student for about <b>five years</b>. The role also feels different in relation to patients, lecturers and supervising doctors.","w":["Temporary.","Dynamic.","Acquired.","Not only."],"k":"Roles: dynamic, temporary."},{"id":22005,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Being someone's daughter is an example of which type of role (as opposed to acquired)?","e":"Ascribed roles are given (e.g. daughter); acquired roles are achieved (e.g. doctor).","k":"Ascribed.","a":["ascribed","ascribed role"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of social role?","What are the 2 types of social role?","Ascribed; Acquired"]]},{"id":22006,"t":"Social Roles","s":"What does competent role performance require?","o":["Actors recognising role rights and responsibilities","Actors who follow written legal contracts and nothing else","Actors who ignore social expectations","Fixed hierarchies that never change","Avoiding relationships with patients"],"e":"Competent role performance requires <b>“actors”</b> who recognise role <b>rights and responsibilities</b> (e.g. patients and doctors). Awareness of roles shapes social expectations; <b>power</b> plays a role.","w":["Not only legal.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Actors recognise rights/responsibilities.","rn":[["","What does competent role performance require?","What does competent role performance require?","\"Actors\" recognising rights, responsibilities; e.g. patients and doctors"]]},{"id":22007,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Heaven et al. (2013) studied interventions for retired people. What did they find?","o":["Meaningful roles (e.g. volunteering) benefited health and well-being","Meaningful roles (e.g. volunteering) harmed health through stress","Retirement alone improved health in most people","Roles had no measurable effect on health","Retired people stopped seeking healthcare"],"e":"On <b>retirement</b> people may lose status and health-seeking behaviour changes (more GP visits, thinking about being sick). <b>Heaven et al. (2013)</b>: keeping meaningful roles (voluntary roles) gave <b>benefits to health and well-being</b>.","w":["Benefited.","Not.","Not.","More GP visits."],"k":"Meaningful roles help retirees.","rn":[["","Why can retirement affect health?","Why can retirement affect health?","Loss of an important role; Less status in society"],["","What happens if no meaningful alternative role is found?","What happens if no meaningful alternative role is found?","Life may feel empty"],["","What are secondary gains?","What are secondary gains?","Benefits making sick role attractive"]]},{"id":22008,"t":"The Doctor Role","f":"hp07","s":"Which two kinds of rights and obligations are correct?","o":["Legal rights and moral commitments","Legal rights and financial duties","Moral commitments and religious duties","Political rights and social duties","Contractual rights and penalties"],"e":"Two icons: <b>legal rights</b> (scales – e.g. a child's legal and human rights) and <b>moral commitments</b> (handshake – e.g. medical students and doctors behaving morally appropriately).","w":["Moral.","Legal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Legal rights + moral commitments.","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of obligations do doctors have to patients?","What 2 kinds of obligations do doctors have to patients?","Legal; Moral"],["","Which role brings legal rights, e.g. in childhood?","Which role brings legal rights, e.g. in childhood?","Child role: legal and human rights"],["","What do medical students and doctors have to uphold morally?","What do medical students and doctors have to uphold morally?","Moral commitments; Behave in a morally appropriate way"]]},{"id":22009,"t":"Social Roles","s":"On placement, a patient asks a student a question they cannot answer. What is the appropriate response?","o":["“Sorry, I don’t know, but I’ll ask my GP supervisor”","“I’m not sure, but it’s probably nothing serious”","Look it up in front of the patient and diagnose","Tell the patient to search online","Ignore the question"],"e":"Challenges include not having an answer to everything. It is perfectly appropriate to say “I'm sorry, I don't know, but I'm going to ask my GP supervisor.” Supervisors prefer students who engage and ask questions.","w":["Don’t guess.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Admit uncertainty, ask supervisor.","rn":[["","What should you say if you do not know a patient's answer?","What should you say if you do not know a patient's answer?","\"I don't know, I will ask my supervisor\""]]},{"id":22010,"t":"Social Roles","s":"After a distressing placement day, a student wants to talk to a friend about it. What is the appropriate approach?","o":["Talk to someone trusted, keeping it fully anonymous","Share full details with a friend to process it","Post about it online without names","Never mention it to anyone at all","Discuss it in the staff kitchen"],"e":"<b>Confidentiality</b> from day one. You can talk to someone trusted if you need to, as long as you keep it <b>very anonymous</b> (not “someone with brown hair, about 67, who lives in this village”). Avoid gossiping in waiting rooms or staff kitchens.","w":["Anonymous.","Not.","Can talk.","Not."],"k":"Anonymise when debriefing.","rn":[["","How can distressing cases be discussed while keeping confidentiality?","How can distressing cases be discussed while keeping confidentiality?","Keep it anonymous: no identifying details; Talk only with a trusted person"]]},{"id":22012,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Why can it be hard for patients to explain what they need in a consultation?","o":["Nerves, power imbalance, language and roles","Doctors usually refuse to listen to what patients are worried about","Patients always already know their diagnosis","Consultations are too long to stay focused","Patients have no role in the consultation"],"e":"It can be difficult to articulate yourself even as educated people – harder with a <b>power imbalance</b> or <b>language barrier</b>. A lot of a consultation is to do with <b>the roles both people are playing</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Roles shape consultations.","rn":[["","What can make explaining your needs to a doctor hard?","What can make explaining your needs to a doctor hard?","Nervousness / anxiety; Power imbalance; Language barrier"]]},{"id":22013,"t":"The Doctor Role","f":"hp10","s":"Which statement about the role of the doctor is correct?","o":["Patient satisfaction affects adherence to advice","Patient satisfaction has little effect on adherence","Doctors must always decide for patients","The doctor’s role is fixed and unchanging","Doctors cannot override everyday social rules"],"e":"Four points: <b>obligations to patients</b>; <b>evolving role</b>; <b>patient satisfaction affects adherence</b>; doctors' <b>rights and obligations override many everyday social rules</b> (e.g. asking people to undress – a privilege).","w":["It does.","Not.","Evolving.","They do."],"k":"Doctor role (1) points.","rn":[["","What does patient satisfaction affect?","What does patient satisfaction affect?","Adherence to medical advice"]]},{"id":22014,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"How has the doctor's role evolved?","o":["Healer → scientist → leader/guide","Scientist (Victorian) → healer/magician → leader/guide","Leader/guide → scientist → healer/magician","Healer/magician → leader/guide → scientist","Unchanged for 500 years"],"e":"About 500 years ago (and still in some cultures) a <b>traditional healer or magician</b>; around <b>Victorian times</b> a <b>scientist</b>; now a <b>leader or guide</b> – sometimes a counsellor or even a <b>spiritual leader</b> as people bring all their issues.","w":["Order.","Reversed.","Order.","Changed."],"k":"Healer → scientist → guide.","rn":[["","How has the role of the doctor evolved over 500 years?","How has the role of the doctor evolved over 500 years?","Magicians and healers; Scientist-treater; Practitioners, guides and leaders"],["","What is the doctor role today?","What is the doctor role today?","Leader or guide; Sometimes like a counsellor or spiritual leader"]]},{"id":22015,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"A patient is very satisfied with how a GP listened to them. What is the likely effect?","o":["Better adherence to advice","They are less likely to return for follow-up","They will need more tests","They will adopt the sick role","No effect on behaviour"],"e":"<b>Patient satisfaction affects adherence</b>: patients are much more likely to follow advice if satisfied with the interaction (linked to communication skills).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Adherence."],"k":"Satisfaction → adherence.","rn":[["","What does patient satisfaction affect?","What does patient satisfaction affect?","Adherence to medical advice"]]},{"id":22016,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which of the following was <b>not</b> listed as a way the balance of power with patients is changing?","o":["Increasing paternalism","Delegation of decision-making power","Doctor–patient partnerships","Expert patients","(All of the others were listed)"],"e":"Changing balance: <b>delegation of power (decision-making)</b>, <b>doctor–patient partnerships</b>, <b>expert patients</b>. The movement is <b>away</b> from paternalism.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Option 1 is not listed."],"k":"Power shifting to partnership.","rn":[["","What is paternalism in medicine?","What is paternalism in medicine?","Doctor decides who is ill, and how ill they may be"]]},{"id":22017,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"“You do not cross them” is a quote from research about what?","o":["Doctors’ views of power within the team (Crowe et al., 2017)","Patients’ views of doctors’ power (Crowe et al., 2017)","Nurses’ views of patients’ rights","Carers’ views of the state","Teenagers’ views of parents"],"e":"“<b>You do not cross them</b>” is from research into doctors' narratives of power relations in specialist training (Crowe et al., 2017). Power imbalances exist within the team too (e.g. a surgeon who slammed a clinic door).","w":["Doctors on doctors.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"“You do not cross them” = team hierarchy.","rn":[["","Which quote describes power between doctors?","Which quote describes power between doctors?","\"You do not cross them\""]]},{"id":22018,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"Which consultation features convey a power imbalance?","o":["Stethoscope; big swivel chair vs small plastic chair","A white coat and tie, and a large desk between doctor and patient","Sitting at eye level and offering choices","Introducing yourself by first name","A shared table and equal chairs"],"e":"Symbols of authority: <b>stethoscope</b> round the neck; doctor's <b>bigger swivel chair</b> vs patient's <b>small plastic chair</b> (no white coats now). We should try to <b>flatten the hierarchy</b> and become partners.","w":["No white coats now.","Flattening.","Flattening.","Flattening."],"k":"Symbols of authority.","rn":[["","What are symbols of a doctor's authority?","What are symbols of a doctor's authority?","Stethoscope around the neck; Bigger chair than the patient's; White coat (in the past)"]]},{"id":22019,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"A doctor having stitches after childbirth felt disempowered when the obstetrician didn't introduce herself or offer anaesthetic. What point does this illustrate?","o":["A power imbalance exists even for doctors as patients","A power imbalance exists only for non-medical patients","Doctors never feel disempowered as patients","Obstetricians always introduce themselves","Patients should challenge all care"],"e":"“There's always a power imbalance”, even when the patient is a doctor. Doctors are experts, but <b>patients are the only experts in themselves</b>.","w":["Always.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Power imbalance persists.","rn":[["","Is there a power imbalance when a doctor becomes a patient?","Is there a power imbalance when a doctor becomes a patient?","Yes, always; Even a doctor can feel disempowered"]]},{"id":22021,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a feature of Parsons' (1975) sick role?","o":["The patient is responsible for becoming sick","The right to relinquish other roles and responsibilities","The obligation to strive to recover","Doctors are gatekeepers of the sick role","Secondary gains may make it attractive"],"e":"Parsons' sick role: the person is <b>not responsible</b> for assuming it; <b>right to relinquish</b> other roles; <b>obligation to strive to recover</b> (sickness as temporary); doctors are <b>gatekeepers</b>; <b>secondary gains</b> may make it attractive. Memory aid: 2 rights, 1 obligation, 1 gatekeeper, 1 temptation.","w":["Feature.","Feature.","Feature.","Feature."],"k":"Parsons' sick role features.","rn":[["","Is a person responsible for assuming the sick role?","Is a person responsible for assuming the sick role?","No, not responsible for being sick"]]},{"id":22022,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"A GP signs a two-week fit note after surgery. Name the doctor's role in Parsons' sick role that this illustrates.","e":"Doctors act as gatekeepers of the sick role; a criticism is why doctors should decide who may be sick.","k":"Gatekeeper.","a":["gatekeeper","gatekeeping","gatekeepers","gate keeper"],"rn":[["","How do doctors act as gatekeepers of the sick role?","How do doctors act as gatekeepers of the sick role?","Decide if it is okay to be sick; e.g. signing medical certificates"]]},{"id":22023,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which are examples of secondary gains of the sick role?","o":["Attention, state money, needed time off work","Attention, promotion at work, legal exemption","Free transport and housing","Higher self-esteem only","No real gains"],"e":"<b>Secondary gains</b>: more <b>attention</b>; <b>money from the state</b> (long-term illness); <b>time off work</b> really needed because of stress.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Exist."],"k":"Secondary gains examples.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of secondary gains?","What are 3 examples of secondary gains?","More attention; Money from the state; Time off work"]]},{"id":22024,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"A hospital inpatient must wear a gown that doesn't close and ask for help to the toilet. Which sick-role feature does this reflect?","o":["Relinquishing other roles, including dignity","Striving to recover as quickly as possible to leave hospital","Gatekeeping by the ward staff","Secondary gains from hospital care","Role conflict between home and hospital"],"e":"Relinquishing other roles: in hospital you also give up some <b>dignity</b> (gowns, bedpans, asking for help), and roles where you were powerful and important.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Relinquishing roles and dignity.","rn":[["","What can a sick person have to give up?","What can a sick person have to give up?","Dignity (e.g. hospital gown, bedpan); Powerful roles; Independence; must ask for help"]]},{"id":22025,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which criticism of the sick role is most relevant to a patient with type 1 diabetes?","o":["It doesn’t fit self-managed long-term conditions","It doesn’t fit acute infections, where people recover quickly with treatment","It gives diabetes patients no rights at all","It lets doctors set every treatment goal","It assumes secondary gains don’t exist"],"e":"Criticisms (discredited somewhat from the 1990s): <b>long-term conditions</b>; living as <b>normal a life as possible</b>; <b>self-management</b>; <b>patients as experts</b>; <b>patient goals</b>; <b>partnerships</b>; move away from <b>paternalism</b>. With asthma or diabetes it is hardly your fault and you aim to live normally.","w":["Fits acute illness.","Not.","Criticism.","Not."],"k":"Sick role ill-fits chronic disease.","rn":[["","Why does the sick role fit long-term conditions badly?","Why does the sick role fit long-term conditions badly?","Illness not temporary; Not their fault; They need extra support"],["","When was the sick role discredited, and why?","When was the sick role discredited, and why?","1990s; it ignored long-term conditions"]]},{"id":22026,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"When did Parsons' sick role start to be somewhat discredited?","o":["In the 1990s","In the 1950s","In 2020","It never was","In the Victorian era"],"e":"Parsons' research was widely accepted, but in the <b>1990s</b> it started to be <b>discredited</b> a little.","w":["Not.","Not.","It was.","Not."],"k":"Criticised from 1990s.","rn":[["","When was the sick role discredited, and why?","When was the sick role discredited, and why?","1990s; it ignored long-term conditions"]]},{"id":22027,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"How might losses from chronic illness affect recovery?","o":["Losses harm well-being, and so recovery","Lost work and activities speed recovery by forcing people to rest","Losses affect finances but not mental well-being","Losses have no psychological effect","Losses improve self-esteem"],"e":"People may miss work, be unable to look after children or join activities. All that loss affects <b>mental well-being</b>, which impacts <b>ability to recover</b>.","w":["Harm.","Both.","They do.","Not."],"k":"Loss → well-being → recovery."},{"id":22028,"t":"The Carer Role","s":"Name the role of someone who provides unpaid care to a person they have a social relationship with.","e":"Informal carers are common, typically short-term and typically a female relative; the role brings role strain and conflict.","k":"Informal carer.","a":["informal carer","carer","unpaid carer","informal caregiver","caregiver"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 typical features of the informal carer role?","What are the 3 typical features of the informal carer role?","Common; Typically short duration; Typically female (relative)"],["","What is the informal carer role?","What is the informal carer role?","Unpaid care for dependent persons; By someone socially related"]]},{"id":22029,"t":"The Carer Role","s":"Which features typically characterise informal carers?","o":["Common, typically short, typically a female relative","Rare, typically lifelong, typically male","Common, typically lifelong, typically a professional","Rare, typically short, typically a child","Common, typically short, typically a spouse only"],"e":"Informal carer role: <b>common</b>; typically <b>short</b> (e.g. a year or two caring for a parent); typically <b>female (relative)</b>. The true number is unknown because it is so informal.","w":["Common, female.","Short.","Not.","Female relative."],"k":"Carers: common, short, female.","rn":[["","What are the 3 typical features of the informal carer role?","What are the 3 typical features of the informal carer role?","Common; Typically short duration; Typically female (relative)"]]},{"id":22032,"t":"The Carer Role","s":"Why is the care-giving role associated with stress?","o":["It is demanding, causing role strain and conflict","It is demanding, but rarely affects other roles","It is well paid, but very demanding","It is short and easy for most carers","It removes all other roles"],"e":"Care-giving is demanding → <b>role strain/stress + role conflict</b>; it can strain the relationship itself.","w":["Conflict.","Unpaid.","Demanding.","Not."],"k":"Caring → strain + conflict.","rn":[["","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","Role strain/stress; Role conflict"]]},{"id":22033,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A medical student cares for a parent at weekends and works a part-time job, and feels torn between them. Name this concept.","e":"Role conflict: competing demands of several roles cause stress (role strain).","k":"Role conflict.","a":["role conflict","inter-role conflict","interrole conflict"],"rn":[["","What is role conflict?","What is role conflict?","Different role obligations conflict; Competing demands"],["","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","Role strain/stress; Role conflict"]]},{"id":22034,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"What is the definition of psychological stress quoted (Lazarus and Folkman, 1984)?","o":["A relationship appraised as exceeding one’s resources","A person–environment relationship that is harmful whatever the appraisal","Any unpleasant event regardless of appraisal","A surge of stress hormones","A diagnosable mental illness"],"e":"Psychological stress: “a <b>relationship between the person and the environment</b> that is appraised by the person as <b>taxing or exceeding his or her resources</b> and endangering his or her well-being”.","w":["Appraisal.","Appraisal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Stress = appraised mismatch.","rn":[["","What is psychological stress?","What is psychological stress?","Appraised as exceeding your resources; Endangers well-being"]]},{"id":22035,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A new student on the ward feels like “a spare part”, unsure what they're supposed to do. Which source of role stress is this?","o":["Ambiguous expectations","Unrealistic expectations","Conflicting expectations","Inter-group conflict","Role loss"],"e":"Sources: <b>unrealistic</b>, <b>conflicting</b>, <b>ambiguous</b> expectations, <b>inter-group conflict</b> (U-C-A-I). Ambiguous: asked to do something without understanding your role – especially stressful for high achievers.","w":["Impossible demands.","Competing demands.","Different group expectations.","Not."],"k":"“Spare part” = ambiguous.","rn":[["","What are ambiguous expectations as a source of stress?","What are ambiguous expectations as a source of stress?","Unclear what your role requires; e.g. feeling a \"spare part\" on the ward"],["","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","Unrealistic expectations; Conflicting expectations; Ambiguous expectations; Inter-group conflict"]]},{"id":22036,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A manager asks an employee to complete more work than is physically possible. Which source of stress applies?","o":["Unrealistic expectations","Ambiguous expectations","Inter-group conflict","Role loss","Secondary gains"],"e":"<b>Unrealistic</b>: “It's not possible for me to do all the things you're asking me to do.”","w":["Unclear.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Impossible demands = unrealistic.","rn":[["","What are unrealistic expectations as a source of stress?","What are unrealistic expectations as a source of stress?","Not possible to do everything asked"],["","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","Unrealistic expectations; Conflicting expectations; Ambiguous expectations; Inter-group conflict"]]},{"id":22037,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"What is a role set?","o":["Linked roles, e.g. doctor + patient","A single role with many duties","A set of written rules","A personality profile","A carer’s allowance"],"e":"<b>Role sets</b>, e.g. doctor + patient.","w":["Linked.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Role set = linked roles.","rn":[["","What is a role set?","What is a role set?","e.g. doctor + patient"]]},{"id":22038,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","f":"hp22","s":"Which of the following is listed as an example of role loss?","o":["Becoming a parent","Starting a new hobby","Going on holiday","Joining a gym","Moving house"],"e":"Role loss examples: <b>retirement</b>, <b>redundancy</b>, <b>illness</b>, <b>recovery from long-term illness</b>, <b>becoming a carer</b>, <b>ceasing to be a carer</b>, <b>becoming a parent</b>, <b>bereavement</b>. Becoming a parent adopts a role but also loses one.","w":["Not listed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Role loss examples.","rn":[["","Why is becoming a parent also role loss?","Why is becoming a parent also role loss?","Adopt new role but lose the old one; Can lose sense of self"]]},{"id":22039,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"Why is “recovery from long-term illness” considered a form of role loss?","o":["They lose the patient role tied to identity","They only gain roles, so recovery cannot involve any loss","Recovery causes bereavement","Recovery has no effect on roles","It is not considered role loss"],"e":"Listed among role loss examples. Important social roles become <b>central to identity</b>; losing any role (even the sick role) can disrupt sense of self.","w":["Loss.","Not.","Not.","It is."],"k":"Recovery can be role loss.","rn":[["","Why does role loss hurt?","Why does role loss hurt?","Important roles shape identity"],["","Why is becoming a parent also role loss?","Why is becoming a parent also role loss?","Adopt new role but lose the old one; Can lose sense of self"]]},{"id":22040,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A man made redundant cannot find another job and says “who am I if I can't work?” What consequences of role loss are likely?","o":["Lost sense of self, isolation, possible depression","Raised self-esteem from having much more free time","Secondary gains only","No effect if financially secure","Positive feedback"],"e":"Roles provide goals, plans, relationships and reputation – a <b>meaningful sense of self</b>. Without alternatives, life may feel <b>empty or meaningless</b>: ? loss of sense of self, social isolation, ? <b>depression</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Role loss → isolation, depression.","rn":[["","What are 3 possible consequences of role loss?","What are 3 possible consequences of role loss?","Loss of sense of self; Social isolation; Depression"]]},{"id":22042,"t":"The Carer Role","s":"A woman caring full-time for a parent with dementia gives up her job. Which combination of concepts applies?","o":["Carer role, role loss, strain/conflict, money stress","The sick role, secondary gains and gatekeeping by her GP","Ascribed doctor role","Downward comparison only","Fundamental attribution error"],"e":"Informal carers often can't have other jobs (dementia patients can't be left alone): caring brings role strain/conflict, role loss (work) and financial hardship (39% struggling; 43% health affected).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Carer: loss, conflict, strain.","rn":[["","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","Role strain/stress; Role conflict"],["","How does caring affect the relationship with the person cared for?","How does caring affect the relationship with the person cared for?","Role strain and conflict between roles; e.g. caring for a parent"]]},{"id":22043,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"Why is a doctor's ability to ask patients to undress described as “a privilege”?","o":["Rights override social rules; patients trust them","It is a legal requirement for every physical examination","It is a secondary gain of being a doctor","It shows paternalism is ideal","It is a form of role loss"],"e":"Doctors' rights and obligations override many everyday social rules – asking people to undress is “not normal” – yet people trust them; a <b>privilege</b> with responsibilities.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Overriding social rules = privilege.","rn":[["","What do doctors rights and obligations override?","What do doctors rights and obligations override?","Many everyday social rules"]]},{"id":22044,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"A patient with chronic asthma wants to set their own treatment goals. Which modern approach supports this against Parsons' model?","o":["Partnership; patients as experts","Strict gatekeeping of the sick role by the doctor","Paternalism, with the doctor setting goals","Treating the sick role as temporary","Ignoring patient goals"],"e":"Patients are experts in their own illness and should set their own goals: doctor–patient partnerships, away from paternalism.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Partnership, expert patients.","rn":[["","What 3 changes shift the balance of power with patients?","What 3 changes shift the balance of power with patients?","Delegation of decision-making power; Doctor-patient partnerships; Expert patients"],["","Why is it criticised that doctors decide who may be sick?","Why is it criticised that doctors decide who may be sick?","Patients are experts in their own illness"]]},{"id":22045,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which two features of the sick role would a person with a long-term condition find hardest to satisfy?","o":["Striving to recover; sickness as temporary","Not being held responsible for becoming ill","Relinquishing other roles","Seeing a doctor","Receiving attention"],"e":"Chronic illness doesn't fit the <b>obligation to recover</b> or the idea of sickness as <b>temporary</b>.","w":["Fits.","Fits.","Fits.","Fits."],"k":"Chronic illness vs recovery obligation.","rn":[["","How does the sick role view sickness?","How does the sick role view sickness?","As temporary"]]},{"id":22046,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which is an example of an ascribed role that brings strong social expectations?","o":["A son or daughter expected to succeed","A footballer expected to train every week by the club","A doctor expected to keep confidentiality","A society member expected to attend","An employee expected to meet targets"],"e":"Strong social expectation attaches to roles, e.g. parents' expectations at medical school, which can “weigh heavily”. Son/daughter is ascribed.","w":["Acquired.","Acquired.","Acquired.","Acquired."],"k":"Ascribed roles carry expectations.","rn":[["","Give 3 examples of ascribed roles.","Give 3 examples of ascribed roles.","Son or daughter; Parent; Brother or sister"]]},{"id":22047,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which explains why retirement can change health-seeking behaviour?","o":["Losing status may raise doctor visits","Losing a status-giving role usually makes people healthier","Retirement removes all roles","Retirement increases income","Retirement prevents illness"],"e":"On retirement you go from an important person with a job to someone with less status; people visit the doctor more and think more about being sick.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Retirement → health-seeking.","rn":[["","How does health-seeking change after retirement?","How does health-seeking change after retirement?","Visit the doctor more often; Think more about being sick"]]},{"id":22048,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A student feels pressured to be at home caring for mum, at work and studying. Which source of role stress is this?","o":["Conflicting expectations","Ambiguous expectations","Unrealistic expectations only","Inter-group conflict","Role loss"],"e":"<b>Conflicting expectations</b>: expected to be at home looking after your mum, to go to work and to be a medical student.","w":["Unclear.","Single demand.","Group.","Not."],"k":"Multiple competing roles = conflicting.","rn":[["","What are conflicting expectations as a source of stress?","What are conflicting expectations as a source of stress?","Different roles expect different things at once; e.g. home care, work, studying"],["","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","Unrealistic expectations; Conflicting expectations; Ambiguous expectations; Inter-group conflict"]]},{"id":22049,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"Different members of a clinical team expect different things of a student. Which source of stress?","o":["Inter-group conflict","Unrealistic expectations","Ambiguous expectations","Role loss","Secondary gain"],"e":"<b>Inter-group conflict</b>: different people within a group having different expectations.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inter-group conflict.","rn":[["","What is inter-group conflict as a source of stress?","What is inter-group conflict as a source of stress?","People within a group expect different things"]]},{"id":22050,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"Which statement about the doctor as gatekeeper is a criticism raised in the seminar?","o":["Why should doctors decide who may be sick?","Doctors must always act as gatekeepers of the sick role","Gatekeeping is a secondary gain","Gatekeeping helps chronic patients recover","Gatekeeping is a patient’s legal right"],"e":"Why should doctors decide who is ill and how ill they are allowed to be? Patients are experts and should set their own goals – move away from paternalism.","w":["Criticism is the opposite.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gatekeeping criticised.","rn":[["","Why is it criticised that doctors decide who may be sick?","Why is it criticised that doctors decide who may be sick?","Patients are experts in their own illness"]]},{"id":22051,"t":"The Carer Role","s":"Which statement about the number of informal carers is correct?","o":["The true number isn’t known","It is precisely recorded by the state each year","It is falling year on year","It is mostly men","It is under 1,000"],"e":"It is typically women, typically <b>unseen</b>, and the true number is not really known.","w":["Not.","Not.","Women.","Not."],"k":"Carer numbers unknown.","rn":[["","Is informal care visible?","Is informal care visible?","Typically unseen; True number of carers unknown"]]},{"id":22052,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A GP who is also a mother of three, a lecturer and a wife is stressed when her child is ill on a lecture day. Which concept explains this?","o":["Role conflict","Role loss from being away from work","An ascribed role only","The sick role","Downward comparison"],"e":"The lecturer's own example: she would want to be at the lecture and at home – conflict causes a lot of stress.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Multiple roles → conflict.","rn":[["","What can role conflict lead to?","What can role conflict lead to?","Potential stress"],["","What is role conflict?","What is role conflict?","Different role obligations conflict; Competing demands"],["","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","What does the demanding care-giving role lead to?","Role strain/stress; Role conflict"]]},{"id":22053,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which statement about the power dynamic in social roles is correct?","o":["Sometimes we are powerful, sometimes powerless","Power is shared equally across every social role","Only patients hold power in consultations","Power is fixed from birth","Power is irrelevant to health"],"e":"<b>Power dynamics</b>: in some situations we are more powerful than others; in others we feel very powerless.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Relevant."],"k":"Power varies by role.","rn":[["","How does power vary across roles?","How does power vary across roles?","In some situations we are more powerful; In others we feel powerless"]]},{"id":22054,"t":"The Doctor Role","s":"What is meant by “patients are the only experts in themselves”?","o":["No one has had their condition quite like them","Patients know more medicine than doctors","Patients should diagnose themselves","Doctors should avoid examining patients","Patients should avoid doctors"],"e":"Doctors are experts, but patients are the only experts in themselves – even if 100 people have had the condition, no one has had it quite like them.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Patients' unique expertise.","rn":[["","Who are the only experts in themselves?","Who are the only experts in themselves?","Patients; no one has a condition quite like them"]]},{"id":22055,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which statement about medical students and moral commitments is correct?","o":["They must behave morally, e.g. confidentiality","Moral commitments only begin to apply after graduation","They have legal rights but no moral duties","Moral commitments are optional for students","Confidentiality is a legal right of students"],"e":"Moral commitments: medical students and doctors behave morally appropriately; keeping confidentiality is a moral obligation.","w":["Day one.","Both.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Students have moral commitments.","rn":[["","What do medical students and doctors have to uphold morally?","What do medical students and doctors have to uphold morally?","Moral commitments; Behave in a morally appropriate way"]]},{"id":22057,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"Which statement about the stress response is correct?","o":["A response with bodily, mental, emotional and behavioural parts","A purely physical response to immediate danger","A response that is always beneficial","A response with no physiological effect","A response seen only in carers"],"e":"Stress is a <b>response to a situation</b> resulting in <b>physiological, cognitive, emotional and behavioural</b> responses.","w":["More.","Not.","It has.","Not."],"k":"Stress response components.","rn":[["","What is stress?","What is stress?","A response to a situation; Physiological, cognitive, emotional, behavioural responses"]]},{"id":22059,"t":"Social Roles","s":"Which description of social roles is correct?","o":["Dynamic, temporary or fixed, through interaction","Fixed, permanent and independent of other people","Dynamic, but always lifelong once taken on","Set by law and identical in every culture","Personal traits that do not involve others"],"e":"A social role defines the expectations of how someone is supposed to behave. Features: roles are dynamic; can be temporary or fixed; arise when interaction occurs and we enter relationships; depend on context and culture; involve power dynamics.","w":["Not.","Can be temporary.","Context dependent.","Arise in relationships."],"k":"Part A reading.","rn":[["","Social role features: how do they occur?","Social role features: how do they occur?","Dynamic nature; Temporary or fixed; Through interaction and relationships"]]},{"id":22060,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"A patient with chronic fatigue receives state benefits and extra attention from family. In Parsons' terms, what are these?","e":"Secondary gains (attention, state money, time off work) are benefits of occupying the sick role.","k":"Secondary gains.","a":["secondary gains","secondary gain"],"rn":[["","What are 3 examples of secondary gains?","What are 3 examples of secondary gains?","More attention; Money from the state; Time off work"],["","What are secondary gains?","What are secondary gains?","Benefits making sick role attractive"]]},{"id":22061,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"What three things do attitudes reflect?","o":["What we think, feel and plan to do","What we do, but not what we think or feel","Our genetic predispositions","Our income and social class","What others think of us"],"e":"Attitudes reflect <b>what we think</b>, <b>how we feel</b> and <b>what we plan to do</b> (Think, Feel, Do). They are a <b>central component</b> of many models of behaviour, built over time, enabling rapid efficient choices; they <b>can</b> predict behaviour, usually with <b>congruence</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Attitude = think, feel, do.","rn":[["","What 3 things do attitudes reflect?","What 3 things do attitudes reflect?","What we think; How we feel; What we plan to do"]]},{"id":22062,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"People react instantly to a 1950s-style picture of women in a kitchen. What do these instantaneous reactions show?","o":["Attitudes are implicit, allowing quick safe decisions","Attitudes are always deliberate and carefully reasoned before acting","Attitudes never change once formed","Reactions to images are random","Attitudes matter only in politics"],"e":"We all have <b>instantaneous reactions</b>: attitudes are <b>implicit and innate</b> and allow <b>quick snapshot decisions</b> that can keep us safe (crossing the street, moving from a fire) – “not altogether always a bad thing”. The kitchen picture was “stereotypical” and such images are no longer considered acceptable.","w":["Implicit.","They change.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Attitudes: implicit, quick.","rn":[["","Why do attitudes form quickly for safety?","Why do attitudes form quickly for safety?","Snapshot decisions keep us safe; e.g. walking away from a fire"]]},{"id":22063,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"Smoking was once glamorous (e.g. Mad Men images) but is now socially unacceptable to many. What does this illustrate?","o":["Attitudes can change over time at a societal level","Attitudes rarely change once established in society","Behaviour never follows attitudes","Smoking is now universally avoided","Images have no effect on attitudes"],"e":"Students saw the smoking picture as “cool”/glorified; the lecturer's children now see it as foolish. Smoking in pubs (and once on planes) was allowed; now socially unacceptable, though still glorified in contexts and many vape.","w":["They do.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Societal attitudes change.","rn":[["","What 2 ways do we remove dissonance?","What 2 ways do we remove dissonance?","Change behaviour; Change attitudes"],["","Are attitudes easy to change?","Are attitudes easy to change?","No, long-standing and challenging; But possible"],["","At which 3 levels does behaviour change happen?","At which 3 levels does behaviour change happen?","Individual; Organisational; Policy/macro"]]},{"id":22064,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"Why are attitudes important for governments?","o":["Major challenges are behavioural","Governments mostly ignore attitudes when making policy","Attitudes matter only at the individual level","Only economics shapes behaviour","Attitudes don’t affect health"],"e":"Attitudes are relevant to all areas of life and act at individual, organisational and policy/macro levels. Most government challenges are <b>behavioural</b>. Derek Wanless: we can't keep doing the same thing and expect different results.","w":["Not.","Macro too.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Behavioural challenges.","rn":[["","Which challenges are mostly behavioural for governments?","Which challenges are mostly behavioural for governments?","Crime; Obesity; Smoking; Climate change"]]},{"id":22065,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"What is cognitive dissonance?","o":["A mismatch between attitude and behaviour, causing discomfort","A match between attitude and behaviour, causing comfort","A memory disorder","A type of fear appeal","A stereotype"],"e":"<b>Cognitive dissonance</b>: <b>mismatch</b> between attitudes and behaviour – Festinger: “a feeling of psychological discomfort caused by internal inconsistency”. We are motivated to remove it by <b>changing behaviour or changing attitudes</b>.","w":["Mismatch.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dissonance = mismatch.","rn":[["","What is cognitive dissonance?","What is cognitive dissonance?","Mismatch between attitudes and behaviour"],["","How did Festinger describe cognitive dissonance?","How did Festinger describe cognitive dissonance?","Psychological discomfort; Caused by internal inconsistency"]]},{"id":22066,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"A parent who believes vaccines are harmful encounters media reports that a vaccine is effective. According to Festinger, which response is likely?","o":["Discredit or avoid the source","Change behaviour immediately to match the new evidence","Feel no discomfort at all","Adopt the sick role","Use downward comparison"],"e":"Festinger: people experiencing dissonance are motivated to reduce it – adding beliefs that bolster attitudes, <b>discrediting the source</b>, or <b>avoiding sources</b>; big changes in behaviour/attitude occur only under certain conditions. Social media creates an <b>echo chamber</b>.","w":["Rarely.","Discomfort.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dissonance reduction strategies.","rn":[["","What 3 strategies reduce dissonance without changing behaviour?","What 3 strategies reduce dissonance without changing behaviour?","Add beliefs that bolster attitudes; Discredit the new source; Avoid challenging information"]]},{"id":22067,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"A student says “I'm a really healthy person… I only smoke socially.” Name the uncomfortable attitude–behaviour mismatch this reflects.","e":"Cognitive dissonance (Festinger): a mismatch between attitude and behaviour causes discomfort, often resolved by rationalising.","k":"Cognitive dissonance.","a":["cognitive dissonance","dissonance"],"rn":[["","What is cognitive dissonance?","What is cognitive dissonance?","Mismatch between attitudes and behaviour"]]},{"id":22068,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"What does the Theory of Planned Behaviour suggest?","o":["Attitudes guide behaviour, so can change it","Behaviour guides attitudes, so attitudes can never be changed","Behaviour is random","Only fear changes behaviour","Attitudes follow behaviour only"],"e":"Models such as the <b>Theory of Planned Behaviour</b> suggest attitudes guide behaviour, so <b>changing attitudes can change behaviour</b>; persuasive information may lead to more favourable attitudes.","w":["Reverse.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"TPB: attitudes guide behaviour.","rn":[["","Which model says attitudes guide behaviour?","Which model says attitudes guide behaviour?","Theory of Planned Behaviour"]]},{"id":22069,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"Which characteristics define a behaviour?","o":["An action: observable, specific and measurable","A feeling: observable, specific and measurable","An opinion that guides action","An attitude towards something","A personality trait"],"e":"Behaviour: <b>an action</b>, <b>observable</b>, <b>specific</b>, <b>measurable</b> (A-O-S-M). A behaviour is doing (or not doing); an attitude is thinking/feeling. We can see a man running but not why.","w":["Action.","Attitude.","Attitude.","Not."],"k":"Behaviour = action, observable, specific, measurable."},{"id":22070,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"Why is the attitude–behaviour relationship described as complex?","o":["Context and wider determinants shape behaviour","Attitudes alone always determine what people actually do","Behaviour has no identifiable causes","Only genetics determine behaviour","Context is irrelevant to behaviour"],"e":"Behaviour does not happen in a vacuum: <b>context, environment and structural factors</b> (wider determinants – housing, income, access to health care, health literacy) influence it.","w":["Not alone.","Not.","Not.","Relevant."],"k":"Behaviour shaped by wider determinants.","rn":[["","What 4 wider determinants influence behaviour besides attitudes?","What 4 wider determinants influence behaviour besides attitudes?","Housing; Income; Access to health care; Health literacy"]]},{"id":22071,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","f":"ab11","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> one of the six BPS (2020) principles for population messaging?","o":["Create as much fear as possible","Minimise ‘I’ and emphasise ‘we’","Deliver messages from a trusted credible source","Clearly specify behaviours and outcomes","Avoid unintentional negative consequences"],"e":"BPS six: (1) minimise <b>I</b>, emphasise <b>we</b>; (2) <b>trusted credible source</b>, relatable terms; (3) <b>worry but not fear</b>; (4) identify influences on each behaviour and target <b>all relevant behaviours</b>; (5) <b>specify behaviours and outcomes</b>; (6) avoid <b>unintentional negative consequences</b>.","w":["Principle.","Principle.","Principle.","Principle."],"k":"BPS six principles.","rn":[["","Why create worry but not fear?","Why create worry but not fear?","Fear causes fight or flight; Denial and avoidance"],["","How do people cope with too much fear?","How do people cope with too much fear?","Ignore the message; Criticise the message"]]},{"id":22072,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"During H1N1, low uptake of hand washing was linked to lack of public worry. Which BPS principle does this support?","o":["Create worry but not fear","Create fear rather than worry","Minimise ‘we’ and emphasise ‘I’","Use only fear-based messages","Avoid trusted sources"],"e":"Principle 3: (H1N1) low uptake linked to a <b>lack of public worry</b>; some worry is important, but heightened anxiety can lead to fear and fight-or-flight – <b>fear can lead to denial and avoidance</b>.","w":["Worry.","Reversed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Worry, not fear.","rn":[["","Why create worry but not fear?","Why create worry but not fear?","Fear causes fight or flight; Denial and avoidance"]]},{"id":22073,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"Behavioural drivers are linked to which three factors (COM-B)?","o":["Capability, Opportunity, Motivation","Cost, Outcome and Morality","Choice, Obligation and Money","Culture, Opinion and Mood","Context, Order and Memory"],"e":"Principle 4: behavioural drivers link to <b>Capability</b> to enact (knowledge/skill), <b>Opportunity</b> to enable (norms/resources) and <b>Motivation</b> to perform (desire/emotion/habit) the <b>Behaviour</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Capability, Opportunity, Motivation.","rn":[["","What 3 drivers of behaviour must messaging target?","What 3 drivers of behaviour must messaging target?","Capability to enact; Opportunity to enable; Motivation to perform"]]},{"id":22074,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"A public figure tells people to stay home but is seen not doing so; trust falls. Which BPS principle has been undermined?","o":["A trusted, credible source","Minimise ‘I’ and emphasise ‘we’","Clearly specify behaviours and outcomes","Avoid unintended consequences","Create worry but not fear"],"e":"Principle 2: the messenger must be <b>credible</b>; the message relevant and achievable. We won't believe someone we don't trust – the lecturer's lockdown example.","w":["Different.","Different.","Different.","Different."],"k":"Credible messenger.","rn":[["","What must the messenger be?","What must the messenger be?","Trusted; Credible"]]},{"id":22075,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"Which were unintended consequences of lockdown?","o":["Less exercise, lost education, mental health, child delays","Better fitness, better exam results and less anxiety in young people","Less exercise only, with no other effects","Lost education only","No unintended consequences"],"e":"Principle 6: avoid <b>unintentional negative consequences</b> – lockdown led to less exercise and socialisation, lost education, helped kick-start the <b>mental health crisis</b>; toddlers not talking or potty trained. Know your audience to avoid inequality.","w":["Not.","More.","More.","There were."],"k":"Lockdown side effects.","rn":[["","Unintended consequences of COVID lockdown messaging?","Unintended consequences of COVID lockdown messaging?","Lack of exercise; Lack of socialisation and education; Mental health crisis"]]},{"id":22076,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","f":"ab12","s":"Break down “Stay home, Protect the NHS, Save lives”.","o":["Stay home = behaviour; Protect the NHS = reason; Save lives = outcome","Stay home = reason; Protect the NHS = behaviour; Save lives = outcome","Stay home = outcome; Protect the NHS = reason; Save lives = behaviour","All three are outcomes","All three are behaviours"],"e":"<b>Stay at home</b> = behaviour; <b>Protect the NHS</b> = reason (hospitals overcrowded, staff couldn't cope); <b>Save lives</b> = outcome.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Different.","Different."],"k":"Behaviour, reason, outcome.","rn":[["","Why was \"protect the NHS (National Health Service)\" a reason to stay home?","Why was \"protect the NHS (National Health Service)\" a reason to stay home?","Too many sick would overcrowd hospitals; Staff could not cope"]]},{"id":22078,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"What lesson about public health messaging does the Wellcome trust data illustrate?","o":["Bespoke messages with trusted messengers","One message for everyone, delivered by doctors","Religious leaders should never deliver health messages","Trust is identical across all groups","Healthcare professionals are distrusted by all groups"],"e":"The pandemic illustrates the importance of <b>bespoke and targeted</b> public health messages relevant to a <b>wide range of audiences</b>. Also, grouping all BAME people together may hide differences.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Most trusted."],"k":"Targeted messaging.","rn":[["","What did COVID-19 show about public health messages?","What did COVID-19 show about public health messages?","Need bespoke, targeted messages; Relevant to wide-ranging audiences"]]},{"id":22080,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","f":"ab16","s":"Why was the 1980s “AIDS: Don't die of ignorance” campaign judged weak against BPS principles?","o":["It scared people without saying what to do","It used humour, which undermined it","It specified behaviours too precisely","It used trusted local messengers","It avoided fear altogether"],"e":"“Not really… not great”: very influential but mostly <b>scaring</b> people without being educational – it didn't tell people <b>what to do</b> (use a condom; only in small print).","w":["Not.","It didn’t.","Not.","It used fear."],"k":"AIDS campaign: fear without action.","rn":[["","Which 1980s campaign was criticised for scaring without educating?","Which 1980s campaign was criticised for scaring without educating?","HIV/AIDS (\"Don’t die of ignorance\")"]]},{"id":22081,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"Under which conditions do fear appeals have more impact?","o":["Showing how to act, one-time behaviours, in women","When fear is maximal, for lifelong behaviours, and mainly in men","When people lack skills and confidence","When behaviours must be repeated for life","Only in men"],"e":"A meta-analysis showed a positive effect on attitudes, intentions and behaviours. Fear works when: (1) it conveys <b>how</b> to perform the behaviour; (2) <b>one-time behaviours</b>; (3) <b>gender effect</b> – more effective for <b>women</b> (“prevention-focused”). Also a <b>maximum effective value of fear</b>.","w":["Reverse.","Counterproductive.","One-time.","Women."],"k":"When fear appeals work.","rn":[["","What are the 3 key findings on when fear appeals work?","What are the 3 key findings on when fear appeals work?","Say how to do it; One-time behaviours; More effective for women"]]},{"id":22082,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"Which message would a fear appeal most likely succeed with?","o":["“Book your cervical smear test”","“Exercise three times a week forever”","“Change your whole diet permanently”","“Never drink alcohol again”","“Stop all stress in your life”"],"e":"One-time behaviours: “book your cervical smear” (then none for 3–5 years) or “get your vaccine” works better than “exercise three times a week or you may develop CVD”.","w":["Repeated.","Repeated.","Repeated.","Not."],"k":"Fear: one-time behaviours.","rn":[["","What is an example of a one-time behaviour suited to fear appeals?","What is an example of a one-time behaviour suited to fear appeals?","Vaccination; Cervical smear"]]},{"id":22083,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"When can fear appeals be counterproductive?","o":["Low skills or confidence, or excessive fear","When fear is moderate and the recommended actions are clear","When the behaviour is one-time","When the audience is female","When the message explains how to act"],"e":"Counterproductive if people lack <b>knowledge, skills and confidence</b>, or the message creates <b>too much fear</b> → coping by <b>ignoring or criticising</b> the message (“pull the duvet over their heads”).","w":["Works then.","Works.","Works.","Works."],"k":"Fear backfires: low efficacy, too much fear.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways fear appeals can be counterproductive?","What are the 2 ways fear appeals can be counterproductive?","People lack knowledge, skills, confidence; Message generates too much fear"]]},{"id":22084,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"According to risk perception theories, how does coping efficacy affect response to a threat message?","o":["High efficacy → change; low efficacy → defensive reaction","High efficacy → defensive reaction; low efficacy → change","Efficacy has no effect on response","Low efficacy → change; high → denial","Efficacy affects only men"],"e":"People facing a threat want to counter it; if <b>coping efficacy</b> is high, they may change; if low, they <b>react defensively</b>. Medical students probably have relatively high efficacy.","w":["Swapped.","It does.","Reversed.","Not."],"k":"Efficacy determines response.","rn":[["","High vs low efficacy response to a fear message?","High vs low efficacy response to a fear message?","High: change behaviour; Low: react defensively"]]},{"id":22085,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"Which critique of fear-appeal evidence was raised?","o":["Evidence quality; intention ≠ behaviour","There is no published evidence on fear appeals","Fear appeals always fail in practice","Fear was measured perfectly in studies","Only men were studied"],"e":"<b>Critics</b>: reviews have not considered <b>quality of the evidence</b> (random assignment? control group? were people fearful? objective vs subjective behaviour?). An intention to change ≠ behaviour change: the <b>intention–behaviour gap</b>.","w":["There is.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Fear evidence critique.","rn":[["","Which questions test the quality of fear appeal evidence?","Which questions test the quality of fear appeal evidence?","Random assignment to groups?; Control group?; Was fear manipulated and measured?; Behaviour measured objectively?"]]},{"id":22086,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"What are the three major components of fear appeals?","o":["Message, audience, recommended behaviour","The message, the messenger and the medium","Fear, anger and sadness","Cost, time and effort","Image, sound and text"],"e":"Fear appeals have three components: <b>message</b>, <b>audience</b>, <b>recommended behaviour</b>; a contentious topic in health psychology.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Fear appeal components.","rn":[["","What are the 3 major components of a fear appeal?","What are the 3 major components of a fear appeal?","The message; The audience; The recommended behaviour"]]},{"id":22087,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"What is the “maximum effective value of fear”?","o":["Beyond moderate fear, no extra benefit","More fear always works better","Fear must be kept at zero","Fear only works above a maximum","A legal limit on fear in adverts"],"e":"Moderation analyses (Tannenbaum et al.): a <b>maximum effective value</b> – beyond moderate fear, no further benefit (people run, hide, ignore).","w":["Not.","Moderate.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Moderate fear only.","rn":[["","What happens beyond a moderate amount of fear?","What happens beyond a moderate amount of fear?","No further benefit"]]},{"id":22088,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A patient keeps a diary of what happens just before they binge on snacks. Name the behaviour change technique.","e":"Information about antecedents identifies triggers of a behaviour.","k":"Information about antecedents.","a":["information about antecedents","antecedents","self-monitoring of antecedents"],"rn":[["","How can patients use antecedents?","How can patients use antecedents?","Record what precedes the behaviour; e.g. before snacking, smoking"],["","What is an antecedent?","What is an antecedent?","Reliably predicts a behaviour; Comes before it"]]},{"id":22089,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A GP asks a smoker to total their weekly spend × 52 and imagine a family holiday instead. Name this behaviour change technique.","e":"Pros and cons (decisional balance) weighs the benefits and costs of changing.","k":"Pros and cons.","a":["pros and cons","decisional balance","pros and cons decisional balance"],"rn":[["","What tool lists pros and cons?","What tool lists pros and cons?","Decisional balance table"]]},{"id":22090,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A GP suggests thinking of stair-climbing and carrying shopping as reducing sedentary behaviour rather than “doing more exercise”. Which technique?","o":["Framing/reframing","Salience","Antecedents","Health consequences","Comparative imaging"],"e":"<b>Framing/reframing</b>: suggest a new perspective, e.g. everyday tasks as reducing sedentary behaviour; “how could you fit exercise into your week?”","w":["Images.","Triggers.","Facts.","Futures."],"k":"New perspective = reframing.","rn":[["","What is framing/reframing?","What is framing/reframing?","Adopt a new perspective on a behaviour; To change thoughts or emotions about it"]]},{"id":22091,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A grandparent who smokes wants to run around with grandchildren. A GP helps them recognise this conflict. Which technique?","o":["Incompatible beliefs (dissonance)","Pros and cons","Information about antecedents","Salience of consequences","Framing/reframing"],"e":"<b>Incompatible beliefs</b>: discrepancies between behaviour and self-image create discomfort (dissonance), e.g. wanting an active life with grandchildren but still smoking.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Behaviour vs self-image = incompatible beliefs.","rn":[["","Grandparent who smokes but wants to run with grandchildren: which technique?","Grandparent who smokes but wants to run with grandchildren: which technique?","Incompatible beliefs (cognitive dissonance)"]]},{"id":22092,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"Pictures of diseased lungs on cigarette packs use which behaviour change technique?","e":"Salience of health consequences makes outcomes vivid; fear appeals work best with a clear recommended action and high coping efficacy.","k":"Salience of health consequences.","a":["salience of health consequences","salience of consequences","salience"],"rn":[["","How is salience of health consequences increased?","How is salience of health consequences increased?","Images/text to make them memorable; e.g. pictures of diseased lungs"]]},{"id":22093,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"How many behaviour change techniques are in Michie and colleagues' taxonomy (v1)?","e":"BCT Taxonomy v1 lists 93 techniques.","k":"93.","a":["93","ninety-three","ninety three"],"rn":[["","How many behaviour change techniques did Michie identify?","How many behaviour change techniques did Michie identify?","93"]]},{"id":22094,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"Dry January's website includes testimonies, information on sleep and money benefits, and the message that “you're not alone”. Which techniques/principles does it use?","o":["‘We’, consequences, futures, steps, anecdotes","Fear appeals alone, with graphic warnings","Antecedents alone","Legal threats","No recognised techniques"],"e":"Feedback: antecedents/associations; collective <b>“we”</b>; info on <b>physical and emotional effects</b>; <b>imagined futures</b> (sleep, relationships, money); practical steps; <b>anecdotes/testimonies</b>. Individual behaviour change.","w":["More.","More.","Not.","Several."],"k":"Dry January techniques."},{"id":22095,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"What is the danger of a dependent drinker stopping alcohol suddenly?","o":["Dependent drinkers can die stopping suddenly","Stopping alcohol suddenly is always safe","Only light drinkers are at risk","Dry January is dangerous for everyone","Alcohol has no withdrawal effects"],"e":"A student noted the site warns people can die if they stop suddenly; the lecturer confirmed people with <b>alcohol dependence</b> can die if they stop suddenly.","w":["Not.","Dependent.","Not.","It has."],"k":"Alcohol dependence: sudden stop dangerous.","rn":[["","Why can stopping drinking suddenly be dangerous?","Why can stopping drinking suddenly be dangerous?","People with alcohol dependence can die"]]},{"id":22096,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"What is self-esteem and how is it described?","o":["Self-evaluations on a dynamic spectrum","Feelings about ourselves that are fixed from birth onwards","Other people’s opinions of us","Our intelligence","A diagnosis"],"e":"<b>Self-esteem</b> = feelings and evaluations about ourselves; on a <b>spectrum</b> and <b>dynamic</b> (Suls et al., 2002) – influenced in teenage years by hormones, friends, exams, family.","w":["Dynamic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Self-esteem dynamic.","rn":[["","What is self-esteem?","What is self-esteem?","Feelings, evaluations about ourselves"]]},{"id":22097,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"A patient says “Thank goodness I'm not like him – I'll never be that unhealthy.” Name this type of social comparison.","e":"Downward comparison with someone worse off protects self-esteem.","k":"Downward comparison.","a":["downward comparison","downward social comparison","downward"],"rn":[["","What is a downward comparison?","What is a downward comparison?","Comparing with those worse off"],["","What are the 2 types of social comparison?","What are the 2 types of social comparison?","Downward; Upward"]]},{"id":22098,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"What is important for protecting self-esteem when we are criticised?","o":["Affirm positive aspects of ourselves","Accept all criticism silently","Compare only upwards with others","Avoid all feedback","Blame other people"],"e":"It is important for self-esteem to <b>affirm positive aspects</b> of ourselves when criticised.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Affirm positives when criticised.","rn":[["","What helps self-esteem when we are criticised?","What helps self-esteem when we are criticised?","Affirming positive aspects of ourselves"]]},{"id":22099,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"Name the concept describing whether a person sees the cause of their illness as internal or external.","e":"Health locus of control: internal (my actions) vs external (luck, others); external beliefs may reduce engagement with treatment.","k":"Health locus of control.","a":["health locus of control","locus of control"],"rn":[["","What is health locus of control?","What is health locus of control?","How someone perceives illness cause"]]},{"id":22100,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"“I passed because I practised hard; I failed because the examiner was unfair.” Name this bias.","e":"Self-serving bias: internal attribution for success, external for failure.","k":"Self-serving bias.","a":["self-serving bias","self serving bias","self-serving attribution bias"]},{"id":22101,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"A patient believes their illness is due to bad luck and nothing they do will help. How might this affect treatment?","o":["It may reduce engagement; show what they control","An external locus guarantees good adherence to the plan","It has no effect on treatment","It is the fundamental attribution error","It shows they have adopted the sick role"],"e":"A patient not responding to a plan may believe the cause is <b>external</b>; we try to show them it is (internally) within their control.","w":["Not.","It does.","Not.","Not."],"k":"External locus → lower engagement."},{"id":22102,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"What is the fundamental attribution error?","o":["Attributing others’ situations to disposition, not circumstance","Attributing others’ situations to circumstance, not disposition","Attributing our own failures to ourselves","Comparing ourselves upwards","A diagnostic error from tests"],"e":"<b>FAE</b>: we attribute <b>others'</b> situations (unemployed, unhealthy, depressed) to <b>internal/dispositional</b> causes rather than <b>external/situational</b> ones (Ross, 1977). It can affect clinical care via the doctor–patient relationship.","w":["Reversed.","Self.","Not.","Not."],"k":"FAE definition.","rn":[["","What is the fundamental attribution error?","What is the fundamental attribution error?","Blaming others' situations on them; Not on external causes"]]},{"id":22103,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"A doctor assumes an obese patient is lazy, ignoring that they live in a food desert with no safe green space. Name this error.","e":"The fundamental attribution error attributes others' situations to disposition rather than circumstance; clinicians must consider structural barriers.","k":"Fundamental attribution error.","a":["fundamental attribution error","fae","correspondence bias"],"rn":[["","What is the fundamental attribution error?","What is the fundamental attribution error?","Blaming others' situations on them; Not on external causes"],["","Who described the fundamental attribution error?","Who described the fundamental attribution error?","Ross (1977)"]]},{"id":22104,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"Patients with which cancers may be treated less favourably because they are blamed for their illness?","o":["Liver cancer and head and neck cancer","Breast cancer and prostate cancer","Childhood leukaemia","Melanoma","Bowel cancer"],"e":"A <b>hierarchy in cancer</b>: more judgement with liver cancer (alcohol) or head and neck cancers (smoking); these patients are treated less favourably – “That's not okay.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Liver/throat cancer stigma.","rn":[["","Which 2 cancers are treated less favourably by health professionals?","Which 2 cancers are treated less favourably by health professionals?","Liver cancer; Throat (head and neck) cancer"]]},{"id":22105,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"Why must clinicians consider external and situational factors when patients don't follow advice?","o":["Structural barriers shape behaviour","Patients who don’t follow advice are usually lazy or unmotivated","External factors rarely matter","It saves consultation time","It is a legal requirement"],"e":"Motivation is heavily influenced by wider context: a highly motivated person may face many <b>structural barriers</b>. If a patient hasn't done what you asked, <b>consider these factors</b>.","w":["FAE.","They do.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Consider structural barriers."},{"id":22106,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"Name the term for judging someone before having the facts, often based on inaccurate stereotypes.","e":"Prejudice: pre-judgement, often based on stereotypes.","k":"Prejudice.","a":["prejudice"],"rn":[["","What is prejudice commonly based on?","What is prejudice commonly based on?","Inaccurate stereotypes of group members"],["","What is prejudice?","What is prejudice?","Judging before having relevant facts"],["","What does history show about stereotypes and prejudice?","What does history show about stereotypes and prejudice?","Many clashes between groups; Based on erroneous stereotypes"]]},{"id":22107,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"What are implicit biases and how do health professionals compare with the general population?","o":["Automatic associations, similar in professionals","Deliberate prejudices that health professionals do not have","Conscious choices; health professionals have more","Found only in patients","Absent in egalitarian people"],"e":"<b>Implicit biases</b>: automatic and unintentional associations. Health professionals' levels are <b>broadly similar to the wider population</b> – “no better than anyone else”. They exist even in egalitarian-minded people (culture matters).","w":["Automatic; similar.","Similar.","Not.","Present."],"k":"Implicit bias = automatic; doctors similar.","rn":[["","What are implicit biases?","What are implicit biases?","Automatic, unintentional associations"]]},{"id":22108,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"According to FitzGerald and Hurst (2017), in which areas does implicit bias appear in healthcare?","o":["Diagnosis, treatment, questions and tests","Billing, appointment times and waiting lists","Hospital food choices","Only treatment recommendations","Nowhere in clinical care"],"e":"Bias appears in <b>diagnosis</b>, <b>treatment recommendations</b>, <b>number of questions asked</b> and <b>number of tests ordered</b>; biases act against those already vulnerable, so <b>disadvantage leads to further disadvantage</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","More.","It does."],"k":"Where bias shows.","rn":[["","What 3 things are biases likely to influence?","What 3 things are biases likely to influence?","Diagnosis; Treatment decisions; Levels of care"],["","What evidence links biases to diagnosis and treatment?","What evidence links biases to diagnosis and treatment?","Correlational evidence"]]},{"id":22109,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"One study found male doctors and nurses were less sympathetic and more frustrated than female staff with which patients?","o":["Self-harming patients in A&E","Elderly patients with dementia","Children with asthma","Pregnant women","Patients with cancer"],"e":"Doctors' socio-demographic characteristics correlate with bias: in one study <b>male</b> doctors and nurses were significantly less sympathetic and more frustrated with <b>self-harming patients in A&E</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Self-harm in A&E bias.","rn":[["","Who were less sympathetic to self-harm patients in A&E?","Who were less sympathetic to self-harm patients in A&E?","Male doctors and nurses"],["","How do male and female staff differ with self-harm patients in A&E (accident and emerge…","How do male and female staff differ with self-harm patients in A&E (accident and emergency)?","Male staff less sympathetic; More frustrated than female staff"]]},{"id":22110,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"What is a big part of the solution to implicit bias in healthcare?","o":["Being aware of our own biases from our worldview","Ignoring patient characteristics entirely","Treating every patient identically by protocol","Avoiding patients unlike ourselves","Accepting that biases cannot be changed"],"e":"A big part of the solution is <b>being aware</b> of our own biases as we start to practise.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Awareness of bias.","rn":[["","What is the first step in tackling our own bias?","What is the first step in tackling our own bias?","Being aware of it"]]},{"id":22111,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"A health campaign says “Get your flu jab: if you do, you are X% less likely to catch flu and protect others.” Which BPS principle does it mainly fulfil?","o":["Specify behaviours and outcomes","Create fear rather than just worry","Minimise ‘we’","Use an untrusted source","Ignore unintended consequences"],"e":"Principle 5: clear behavioural actions and outcomes, e.g. “if you have the COVID vaccine you are this percentage less likely…”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Behaviour + outcome specified.","rn":[["","What must health messages clearly specify?","What must health messages clearly specify?","The behaviours; The related outcomes"]]},{"id":22112,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"A patient tells their GP they know flossing is good but rarely do it. What concept and approach fit?","o":["An attitude–behaviour gap","Congruence between attitude and behaviour","Role loss","A fear appeal is needed","Secondary gains"],"e":"Flossing example: attitude “flossing is good” but often skipped – think about what influences how we <b>actually behave</b> vs how we want to.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Flossing gap.","rn":[["","Why is flossing an example of attitude vs behaviour?","Why is flossing an example of attitude vs behaviour?","Believe flossing is good (reduces gingivitis); But many do not floss twice a day"]]},{"id":22113,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"Who are “persuasive messengers”?","o":["Doctors – trusted messengers","Celebrities – because they reach the widest audience","Politicians – they set health policy","Religious leaders – they are trusted by all groups","No one is persuasive"],"e":"“<b>We are</b>”: doctors are quite persuasive messengers. Healthcare professionals are trusted implicitly because of their role – a privilege.","w":["“We are.”","Not.","Some groups.","Not."],"k":"Doctors are persuasive messengers.","rn":[["","What must the messenger be?","What must the messenger be?","Trusted; Credible"]]},{"id":22115,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"Which pairing of concept and example is correct?","o":["External – “I caught this cold from your cough”","Internal attribution – “I was just unlucky this time”","FAE – “I failed because the test was unfair”","Congruence – smoking while believing it harmful","Upward comparison – “I’m glad I’m not like you”"],"e":"External: “because you had that cough last week I got this cold”. Internal: “I'm healthy because I eat well and exercise”.","w":["External.","Self-serving.","Dissonance.","Downward."],"k":"Attribution examples.","rn":[["","\"It’s your fault I caught this cold\" is which attribution?","\"It’s your fault I caught this cold\" is which attribution?","External"]]},{"id":22117,"t":"Persuasive Messaging","s":"A public health message says “We’re all in this together – let’s protect each other.” Which BPS principle does it mainly use?","o":["Minimise ‘I’ and emphasise ‘we’","Deliver messages from a trusted, credible source","Create worry but not fear","Clearly specify behaviours and outcomes","Avoid unintended negative consequences"],"e":"BPS principles for public health messaging include: (1) minimise ‘I’ and emphasise ‘we’; (2) deliver messages from a trusted credible source; (3) create worry but not fear; (4) behavioural drivers (Capability, Opportunity, Motivation); (5) clearly specify behaviours and outcomes; (6) avoid unintended negative consequences.","w":["No messenger is named.","No worry is created.","No specific behaviour or outcome.","Not the focus."],"k":"Part B reading.","rn":[["","Why emphasise \"we\" not \"I\"?","Why emphasise \"we\" not \"I\"?","Creates a collective viewpoint; We look after each other"]]},{"id":22118,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"A doctor asks a minority-ethnic patient fewer history questions and orders fewer investigations than for others with the same complaint. What does this exemplify?","o":["Implicit bias worsening disadvantage","Good, efficient practice","Respecting patient choice","Cognitive dissonance","Role conflict"],"e":"Some minority ethnic groups are asked <b>fewer questions</b>, have <b>fewer investigations</b> and are offered <b>fewer treatment options</b> – implicit bias.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Fewer questions/tests = bias.","rn":[["","Why are biases against the vulnerable worrying?","Why are biases against the vulnerable worrying?","Disadvantage can lead to further disadvantage"],["","What are implicit biases?","What are implicit biases?","Automatic, unintentional associations"],["","How does doctors’ implicit bias compare with the general population?","How does doctors’ implicit bias compare with the general population?","Similar level"]]},{"id":22119,"t":"Attitudes and Behaviour","s":"At which levels can attitude-change interventions be delivered?","o":["Individual and population levels","Population level only","Individual level only","Through legislation only","Through schools only"],"e":"Changing attitudes is part of health promotion at <b>individual</b> (doctor–patient) and <b>population</b> (public health messaging) levels.","w":["Both.","Both.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Individual and population.","rn":[["","Changing attitudes is part of health promotion at which 2 levels?","Changing attitudes is part of health promotion at which 2 levels?","Individual; Population"]]},{"id":22121,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"A 40-year-old builder has an operation on his knee. His GP signs a certificate giving him two weeks off work, and his family take over his jobs at home. He is keen to do his exercises so he can get back to work quickly. He admits he quite enjoys the extra attention from his family.<br><br>Which features of Parsons' sick role are shown?","o":["Doctor as gatekeeper, relinquishing other roles, obligation to recover and secondary gains","An ascribed role, role conflict and role loss","Cognitive dissonance, fear appeals and framing","Internal locus of control and the fundamental attribution error","Upward and downward comparison and self-affirmation"],"e":"Step 1: Parsons' (1975) sick role says the person is not responsible for being sick, has the right to relinquish other roles, has the obligation to strive to recover, and doctors are gatekeepers of the role. Step 2: the GP's certificate (gatekeeper), family taking his jobs (relinquishing roles), his wish to recover, and enjoying attention (secondary gains that may make the role attractive) all fit.","w":["These are role concepts but not features of the sick role.","These are attitude-change concepts.","These are attribution concepts.","These are self-esteem concepts."],"k":"Sick role: not responsible, relinquish roles, obliged to recover, doctor gatekeeper, secondary gains.","rn":[["","What are secondary gains?","What are secondary gains?","Benefits making sick role attractive"],["","How do doctors act as gatekeepers of the sick role?","How do doctors act as gatekeepers of the sick role?","Decide if it is okay to be sick; e.g. signing medical certificates"],["","What obligation does a sick person have?","What obligation does a sick person have?","To strive to recover"]]},{"id":22122,"t":"The Sick Role","s":"A 35-year-old woman has had type 1 diabetes for 20 years. She manages her own insulin, works full time and sets her own goals with her diabetes team, who treat her as an expert in her condition. A student applies Parsons' sick role to her and expects her to give up her roles until she recovers.<br><br>Why does the sick role fit her poorly?","o":["It assumes sickness is temporary; long-term conditions involve self-management and partnership","She is responsible for her illness, so the role cannot apply","Doctors are no longer gatekeepers of any illness","The sick role applies only to carers","She has role conflict, so the sick role is replaced by the carer role and the obligations that come with it"],"e":"Step 1: the sick role includes an obligation to strive to recover, treating sickness as temporary. Step 2: criticisms include that it does not fit long-term conditions, where people aim to live as normal a life as possible, self-manage, act as expert patients with their own goals, in doctor–patient partnerships that move away from paternalism.","w":["The lecture's criticism is about long-term conditions, not blame.","Doctors are still described as gatekeepers.","The sick role is a role for the sick person, not carers.","She is not described as a carer."],"k":"Sick role criticised for long-term conditions, self-management, expert patients.","rn":[["","Why does the sick role fit long-term conditions badly?","Why does the sick role fit long-term conditions badly?","Illness not temporary; Not their fault; They need extra support"]]},{"id":22123,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A 22-year-old medical student also looks after her mother, who has dementia, and works part time in a café. She is expected to be at home caring, at work and on placement at the same time. On the ward she feels like \"a spare part\" because nobody has explained what she should be doing, which makes her even more stressed.<br><br>Which sources of stress are described?","o":["Conflicting expectations (role conflict) and ambiguous expectations","Unrealistic expectations and inter-group conflict only","Role loss through retirement and redundancy","The fundamental attribution error and implicit bias","Secondary gains from the sick role, which make her want to stay at home rather than go to work or placement"],"e":"Step 1: obligations attached to different roles may conflict (family, work and study), giving competing demands, i.e. role conflict and potential stress from conflicting expectations. Step 2: being asked to do something without understanding the role, such as feeling like a spare part on the ward, is stress from ambiguous expectations.","w":["The main sources described are conflicting and ambiguous expectations.","She has not lost a role.","These are attribution and bias concepts.","She is not in the sick role."],"k":"Role conflict = conflicting expectations; unclear role = ambiguous expectations.","rn":[["","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","What are the 4 sources of stress in managing roles?","Unrealistic expectations; Conflicting expectations; Ambiguous expectations; Inter-group conflict"],["","What are ambiguous expectations as a source of stress?","What are ambiguous expectations as a source of stress?","Unclear what your role requires; e.g. feeling a \"spare part\" on the ward"]]},{"id":22124,"t":"Role Conflict, Stress and Loss","s":"A 66-year-old man retires after 40 years as a headteacher. His job had given him his daily goals, social contacts and a respected reputation in his town. Six months later he says his life feels empty, he rarely sees anyone, and his GP is concerned about his low mood.<br><br>Which concept explains his problems, and what might help?","o":["Role loss; establishing meaningful alternative roles","Role conflict; reducing his competing demands","The sick role; a certificate excusing him from duties","Cognitive dissonance; changing his attitude to retirement","Ascribed role; accepting that roles he was born with cannot change"],"e":"Step 1: role loss (e.g. retirement) matters because important social roles become central to identity, with goals, plans, relationships and reputation giving a valued sense of self. Step 2: if meaningful alternative social roles cannot be established, life may feel empty or meaningless, with loss of sense of self, social isolation and possibly depression; meaningful roles support well-being in retirement.","w":["He has lost a role rather than having competing demands.","He is not sick in Parsons' sense.","Dissonance is a mismatch of attitude and behaviour.","His headteacher role was acquired, not ascribed."],"k":"Role loss → loss of identity, isolation, ?depression; new meaningful roles help.","rn":[["","What happens if no meaningful alternative role is found?","What happens if no meaningful alternative role is found?","Life may feel empty"],["","What are 3 possible consequences of role loss?","What are 3 possible consequences of role loss?","Loss of sense of self; Social isolation; Depression"]]},{"id":22125,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A 60-year-old grandfather says he wants to stay fit so he can run around with his grandchildren, but he still smokes 20 cigarettes a day. He feels uncomfortable when his granddaughter asks why he smokes. His GP plans to use this discomfort to help him quit.<br><br>Which concept and behaviour change technique is the GP using?","o":["Cognitive dissonance; the incompatible beliefs technique","Fundamental attribution error; fear appeals","Secondary gains; pros and cons","Role conflict; framing","Downward comparison; information about antecedents that reliably predict his smoking"],"e":"Step 1: cognitive dissonance is a mismatch between attitudes and behaviour, and people are motivated to remove it by changing behaviour or attitudes. Step 2: the incompatible beliefs technique (Michie et al., 2013) uses discrepancies between behaviour and self-image, such as wanting an active life with grandchildren while still smoking, to motivate change.","w":["FAE is about blaming others' dispositions.","Secondary gains belong to the sick role.","Role conflict is competing role demands.","Downward comparison is a self-esteem strategy."],"k":"Dissonance between values and behaviour → incompatible beliefs technique.","rn":[["","Grandparent who smokes but wants to run with grandchildren: which technique?","Grandparent who smokes but wants to run with grandchildren: which technique?","Incompatible beliefs (cognitive dissonance)"]]},{"id":22126,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"During a pandemic, a public health team designs a campaign: \"Stay at home. Protect the NHS. Save lives.\" The message is delivered by trusted doctors, emphasises what \"we\" can do together, and aims to make people concerned without terrifying them.<br><br>What do the three parts of the slogan represent, and which BPS principles does the delivery follow?","o":["Behaviour, reason and outcome; \"we\" not \"I\", a trusted source, and worry not fear","Antecedent, consequence and reframing; maximum fear","Attitude, belief and stereotype; \"I\" not \"we\"","Behaviour, outcome and reason; delivery by everyday sources such as friends and family, journalists and the media","Three fear appeals; one-time behaviour only"],"e":"Step 1: in the lecture, \"Stay at home\" is the behaviour, \"Protect the NHS\" is the reason for the behaviour, and \"Save lives\" is the outcome. Step 2: the BPS (2020) guidance includes minimising \"I\" and emphasising \"we\", delivering messages from a trusted credible source, and creating worry but not fear.","w":["These are behaviour change techniques, and fear should not be maximal.","The guidance says emphasise \"we\", not \"I\".","The order is behaviour, reason, outcome, and trusted health sources were used.","The slogan is not three fear appeals."],"k":"\"Stay home / Protect NHS / Save lives\" = behaviour / reason / outcome.","rn":[["","When is fear appeal reasoning true?","When is fear appeal reasoning true?","Only in specific, rare circumstances; Assumes confronting harm changes behaviour"]]},{"id":22127,"t":"Fear Appeals","s":"A health campaign shows graphic images of diseased lungs to smokers. It works well for some people, especially those told exactly how to access a quit service. However, smokers who feel they have no confidence or skills to quit, and those who find the images terrifying, ignore or criticise the message.<br><br>Which statement best explains these mixed results?","o":["Fear appeals help when paired with how to act, but backfire with low efficacy or too much fear","Fear appeals always fail because fear never motivates people","Fear appeals work best for repeated daily behaviours","Fear appeals work best in men, who are more prevention-focused than women","Fear appeals work only when they create the maximum possible fear, whatever the person's skills or confidence"],"e":"Step 1: a meta-analysis showed fear appeals can positively affect attitudes, intentions and behaviours, especially when they also convey how to perform the recommended behaviour, for one-time behaviours, and more in women (more prevention-focused). Step 2: they can be counterproductive if people lack the knowledge, skills and confidence to change, or if too much fear makes people ignore or criticise the message.","w":["A certain amount of fear may motivate people.","They are recommended for one-time behaviours.","The gender effect favours women.","Too much fear is counterproductive."],"k":"Fear appeals: work with \"how to\"; backfire with low efficacy or too much fear.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ways fear appeals can be counterproductive?","What are the 2 ways fear appeals can be counterproductive?","People lack knowledge, skills, confidence; Message generates too much fear"],["","How do people cope with too much fear?","How do people cope with too much fear?","Ignore the message; Criticise the message"]]},{"id":22128,"t":"Self-Esteem and Attribution","s":"A doctor sees a 45-year-old unemployed man with obesity and depression. She assumes his problems are due to laziness and poor choices, without asking about his recent redundancy, his area's lack of affordable healthy food or his caring duties. The man feels judged and stops attending.<br><br>Which bias is the doctor showing, and why does it matter?","o":["The fundamental attribution error; it harms the doctor–patient relationship","Self-serving bias; it improves adherence","Cognitive dissonance; it changes her attitude","Upward comparison; it boosts her self-esteem","External locus of control; she blames luck"],"e":"Step 1: the fundamental attribution error is the tendency to attribute others' situations to internal, dispositional causes rather than external, situational causes, e.g. blaming unemployment, poor health or depression on individual behaviour. Step 2: attribution errors can affect clinical care through their impact on the doctor–patient relationship, so external and situational factors must be considered.","w":["Self-serving bias is about our own successes and failures.","Dissonance is a mismatch between her attitude and behaviour.","Upward comparison is about comparing with superiors.","She blamed internal factors, not external ones."],"k":"FAE: blaming others' dispositions, ignoring situations → harms relationship.","rn":[["","What is the fundamental attribution error?","What is the fundamental attribution error?","Blaming others' situations on them; Not on external causes"],["","How do attribution errors affect clinical care?","How do attribution errors affect clinical care?","Through the doctor-patient relationship"]]},{"id":22129,"t":"Stereotypes and Bias","s":"An audit of a hospital emergency department finds that patients from one ethnic group with the same symptoms as others are asked fewer questions, have fewer tests ordered and are less often offered pain relief. The doctors involved deny any conscious prejudice.<br><br>Which concept best explains this, and how does it affect care?","o":["Implicit bias: automatic associations influencing diagnosis, treatment and levels of care","Conscious prejudice only, which doctors are trained to avoid","Stereotypes always being correct rules of thumb","Role conflict between doctors and nurses","The sick role, in which doctors decide who can be excused from duties and who must continue to work"],"e":"Step 1: stereotypes are generalisations that may be correct but are often erroneous, and prejudice is judging before having the relevant facts; implicit biases are automatic and unintentional associations. Step 2: health professionals' clinical judgement and decision making are influenced by implicit biases, which are likely to affect diagnosis, treatment decisions and levels of care.","w":["The doctors deny conscious prejudice; the bias is implicit.","Stereotypes are often erroneous.","The pattern is about patient group, not role conflict.","The sick role is unrelated to biased care."],"k":"Implicit bias: automatic, unintentional; affects diagnosis, tests, treatment.","rn":[["","What are implicit biases?","What are implicit biases?","Automatic, unintentional associations"],["","What 3 things are biases likely to influence?","What 3 things are biases likely to influence?","Diagnosis; Treatment decisions; Levels of care"]]},{"id":22130,"t":"Behaviour Change Techniques","s":"A practice nurse sees a smoker who spends £70 a week on cigarettes. She asks him to multiply this by 52 and imagine the family holiday it could pay for, then to picture himself in a year's time, coughing every morning, versus running around with his children. She also asks him to note what happens just before each cigarette.<br><br>Which three techniques is she using?","o":["Pros and cons, comparative imaging of futures and antecedents","Framing, salience and incompatible beliefs","Fear appeals, emotional consequences and reframing","Downward comparison, upward comparison and affirmation","Health consequences, salience and framing, using pictures of diseased lungs to make the risk memorable"],"e":"Step 1: listing gains such as money saved is the pros and cons technique, and imagining and comparing outcomes after quitting versus not quitting is comparative imaging of futures. Step 2: recording what precedes each cigarette is information about antecedents, which reliably predict behaviour (Michie et al., 2013).","w":["She is not reframing, using images, or highlighting incompatible beliefs.","She does not use fear or reframing.","These are self-esteem strategies.","She does not use health consequences or images."],"k":"Money gains = pros/cons; future pictures = comparative imaging; triggers = antecedents.","rn":[["","What is comparative imaging of futures?","What is comparative imaging of futures?","Imagine and compare outcomes; Quitting vs not quitting"],["","Example of comparative imaging of futures for a smoker?","Example of comparative imaging of futures for a smoker?","Imagine life after stopping; Running around with children"]]}]);
